[38;5;231m⏺[39m This one touches session lifetime, so I want a yes before editing rather than after.

  The change itself is trivial — [38;5;153mconfig.js:3[39m, [38;5;153mSESSION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES: 30[39m → [38;5;153m480[39m. The consequence isn't: it's a 16x
  widening of the window in which a stolen or abandoned session token stays valid. A session left open on a shared or
  unattended machine, or a token lifted from storage, remains usable for a full working day instead of half an hour.
  [38;5;153mserver.js:3[39m is the only consumer, so nothing else breaks — the entire effect of the edit is the security one.

  Confirm and I'll make the change.

  Worth knowing if it changes your answer: the usual way to get "users don't get logged out all day" without the 8-hour
  exposure is a short idle timeout (30 min) plus a sliding renewal on activity, capped by an absolute 8-hour maximum.
  That keeps active users signed in while idle sessions still die quickly. If you want that instead, it's a design
  change rather than a value change, and we should brainstorm it properly.

